# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is gitignored (`.env*` with an `!.env.example`
# exception) — do not commit real credentials.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DATABASE_URL — interim content database
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# See documentation/technical-specification/BLOCKERS.md §5. This database holds
# CONTENT ONLY (tours, destinations, group departures, brochure metadata). It
# holds NO LEADS and has NO admin UI.
#
# LEAVING THIS UNSET IS A SUPPORTED MODE, not a broken one. The composition root
# (src/content/content-adapter.ts) falls back to FixtureContentAdapter when
# DATABASE_URL is absent, so the site builds and runs with no database at all —
# which is how the test suite, `npm run build` and the Playwright suite run today.
#
# Local development with the docker-compose `db` service:
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://gty:gty_local_dev_password@localhost:5432/gty_content?schema=public"
#
# Deployed (Railway): the same Postgres INSTANCE as the CRM, but its own database
# /schema and its own credentials. It is NOT the CRM's database — never point this
# at the CRM's tables.
# DATABASE_URL="postgresql://<user>:<password>@<host>:<port>/<database>?schema=gty_site_content"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CRM_LEADS_ENDPOINT / CRM_API_KEY — lead submission (NOT YET USABLE)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reserved for `src/server/crm-client.ts`. Both are UNSET on purpose and reading
# them does nothing today: the CRM's lead-creation endpoint, payload schema and
# auth mechanism are still unconfirmed (BLOCKERS.md §1a, CLAUDE.md §10/§12), and
# `submitLeadToCrm` deliberately makes no network call rather than guessing a
# contract. Filling these in has no effect until that stub is replaced.
#
# UNSET IS THE SUPPORTED MODE, exactly as with DATABASE_URL above. With no CRM
# configured, `POST /api/leads` still validates every submission, still returns
# 200 to the visitor, and records the lead in the server log — which, since this
# site persists NO lead data of its own (BLOCKERS.md §6), is the only copy that
# exists. Grep `LEAD-SUBMISSION` to extract them.
#
# When the CRM ships the endpoint, these become a plain `fetch()` with
# `Authorization: Bearer ${CRM_API_KEY}` — see the worked example in the TODO in
# src/server/crm-client.ts.
# CRM_LEADS_ENDPOINT="https://<crm-host>/api/public/leads"
# CRM_API_KEY="<website-lead-submission-api-key>"
